  "title": {
    "en": "Plovers, godwits and Whimbrel"
  },
  "subtitle": {
    "en": "Ringed, Little Ringed, Kentish, Grey and Golden Plover, both godwits and the Whimbrel"
  },
  "intro": {
    "en": "Plovers run, stop and look. Their eyes are big, their bills are short and they feed by sight. Godwits and curlews have long bills and probe the mud. Between them the group has some of the most confusing pairs in the field: Ringed and Little Ringed Plover, Grey and Golden Plover, Bar-tailed and Black-tailed Godwit, and Whimbrel and Curlew.\n\nSeason: Ringed Plovers are on the coast all year, with huge numbers of Arctic breeders passing in May and August. Little Ringed Plovers arrive in late March and breed on gravel pits. Golden Plovers come south in autumn to ploughed fields and stay until March, Grey Plovers do the same on the estuaries. Whimbrels pass in April and May and again in July to September.\n\nPairs and tools: for each pair we use one fast clue (leg colour for the ringed plovers, armpit colour for the big plovers, wing bar for the godwits) and one slow one (the voice)."
  },
  "words": [
    {
      "term": {
        "en": "eye ring"
      },
      "explain": {
        "en": "A ring of bare skin around the eye. Bright yellow in Little Ringed Plover."
      }
    },
    {
      "term": {
        "en": "wing bar"
      },
      "explain": {
        "en": "A pale line along the folded or open wing. Ringed Plover has a bold white one; Little Ringed Plover has none."
      }
    },
    {
      "term": {
        "en": "axillaries"
      },
      "explain": {
        "en": "The \"armpit\" feathers under the wing. Black in Grey Plover, white in Golden Plover."
      }
    },
    {
      "term": {
        "en": "breast band"
      },
      "explain": {
        "en": "A dark band across the breast of a plover."
      }
    }
  ],
